A||N||9||prof bobo||Advice for replacing A/C?||||1||
Z||000000||prof bobo||06-08-2000||02:07 AM||||I'm the original owner of a _very tired_ '85 GT that's got 300K+ miles on it, I've had the engine in and out 1/2 a dozen times for clutches, a rebuild, etc.   I'm in the process of stripping, rebuilding, replacing, restoring, etc.  All the body panels are off, the engine's coming out, again, this weekend.  One of my goals is to try to get all traces of rust off the frame in repainting.  The A/C hasn't worked for a long time (since clutch #3), and some of its components are in the way.  While I'm replacing things anyway, I thought it might be nice to have working A/C again.  Anyone have any advice as to what to do towards upgrading to something that works with a refrigerant I _can_ buy?  I'm not opposed to replacing major components, but what's available, and more importantly, best?    ||||reg||1
Z||000001||DiggityDan||06-08-2000||02:59 AM||||If its available to the public...get Freeze-12. I'm not sure if its available or not, my dad has a license so I get it all from him. But if you can get it, but that. People brag about 134.....but honestly, it sucks. Unless you drive mostly on the highway it won't get that cool. <BR>134 works really well while you are driving....but if you stop alot - city driving - it doesn't work very well. I converted mine to Freeze-12 just because its cheaper than R-12 and you don't have to replace all the crap like you do with 134.<BR>Just my thoughts||||reg||1
Z||000002||Terrybogin||06-08-2000||03:16 AM||||Check the EPA's website before using Freeze12 or any other R-12 replacement. The last time I checked (several months ago) there were no R-12 replacements that were legal for automotive use because of flamability issues. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||06-08-2000||07:53 AM||||I did the R-134 retrofit when I had to replace my compressor.  So far so good.<P>I've done it to several of my older cars with no problems.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodrv6||06-08-2000||08:44 AM||||I converted mine to 134 about 3 years ago when I replaced the compressor. The only other components I replaced were the receiver/dryer and the orifice tube.  I cannot tell any difference in the cooling with the 134.  I live in Georgia with it's hot and humid summer weather, and even at a standstill the A/C puts out plenty of cold air. The conversion was around $100 not including the compressor.||||reg||6
Z||000005||mrfixit58||06-08-2000||09:33 AM||||One thing I've done on two cars is replace the annoying 2-bolt securing system (at the compressor)with a 1-bolt. If you notice how the compressor attaches at the top, it has two mounting ears. The factory installed a separate bolt and nut on each ear. This may be fine if the motor is out of the car, you can just stick a wrench in there and tighten the belt with no problems. But, if you blow a belt on the road, the nut and bolt-head in between the ears are impossible to get at with the motor in the car.<P>What I did was to buy a bolt that is long enough to pass through both ears and brackets (about 5") and use just one nut. I took a piece of pipe and measured in between the ears and cut it to fit snuggly. I then thread the bolt through the front ear (closest to the belt), bracket, pipe, rear bracket, and rear ear then the nut. With this set-up, you can loosen the top of the compressor and adjust the tension from underneath the car.<P>Just a (long) suggestion,<BR>Roy||||reg||2
Z||000006||Alex4mula||06-08-2000||01:34 PM||||Very worthy suggestion MRfixit. With the engine still half down when reinstaling my 3.4 it was painful to loose those two damn screws. I thought about that but didn't do it (in hurry to fire it up!). Hope not regreting later...||||reg||1
Z||000007||DiggityDan||06-08-2000||09:59 PM||||Ok...as far as the EPA and Freeze-12 are concerned. It states right on the can that "Freeze-12 is designed specifically to replace R-12 in automotive A/C systems" The fact that the EPA doesn't want you to use it doesn't mean its going to make your car blow up. It means that they want you to use the 134....which, in my opinion sucks ass. Just because the government agency doesn't condone the use of it doesn't mean its not a viable option. I have a friend that has butane in his A?C system..now that is one cold mutha. But anyways, Freeze-12 is comparable to 134 in price per can..but its cheaper because you dont have to change over everything.<BR>This is just my opinion||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||06-10-2000||04:05 PM||||there's more to read than just what is approved on the EPA site.<P>The site list a number of alternate refrigerants, and what they are rated for. It also covers federal rules/laws reguarding conversions and R12 R22 handling.<P>Flamable refrigerants or blends are not MVAC certified for a number of reasons. They are also prohibited by a number of states. The obvious reason is that they can explode.<P>R134 doesn't require as much conversion as many people used to think. Many problems with converted systems have been traced either to improper charging the R134, or to problems that existed before the conversion.<P>The industry accepted rules for replacing a compressor include replaceing the ACC/dryer and orifce tube. Most comressor warantees are voided if this is not done. Many shops also install a filter in the system to catch any loose junk that flushing may have missed.<P>The minimal R134 conversion, assuming the compressor is good:<P>Replace the ACC/Dryer. The desicant in these was only built to last 7-10 years. The desicant removes water and acids that accumulate in the system. If you don't replace it at least drain the old oil and replace the O rings.<P>Replace the Orifice Tube. This insures it's not clogged. Dirt in the orifice will screw up the system no mater what refrigerant is used.<P>drain the old oil from the compressor. One of the problems with conversions is too much oil in the system. Too much oil changes the volume of refrigerant used by the system. It can also mess up the compressor depending on what one it is. (The mineral oil can pool in the copressor sump and keep the new oil from properly lubricating the compressor.)<P>These aren't very expensive things to do. the ACC/dryer is less than $100 and the orifice is about $6. The O rings cost a few dollars for the whole set. You'll need to replace them on any fittings you disconect. (You should replace them all which isn't hard)<P>The whole job can be done in a day.||||reg||1
Z||000009||BOILERMAKER||06-11-2000||04:21 PM||||I just retrofitted my R12 to R134 this afternoon and did it in about 1 hour.  The system was previously open from an accident and it was put back into proper order when I was fixing it up.<P>I replaced the dryer and orifice tube, flushed the system, added oil, then added the refrigerant.  It's cold (I'm sure it could be colder, but for such a small cab, it's great for me) and I am quite satisfied.  This makes the second one that I have done and the other one is working on a year + and is still quite cold.||||reg||1
